my name's lynn wright i'm customer  service team leader  and i manage repairs and proofs i'm now  going to walk you through how our team  restores a business jacket it's an older  style jacket would don't do the strap as  much now  i think it was made in 2005  the jacket has a rip on the right side  of the front and the button needs  replaced  the jacket looks like it's dried out and  it hasn't been re-waxed for years  barber jackets all work so that they are  water resistant  over time the wax will way off so we  recommend that they are sent back to be  re-waxed every year to keep it resistant  to order  firstly i'm going to give it to one of  the operators julianne is given a job  sheet to go along with the jacket  and we'll take the old button off  we use an unpick and unpicks a little  tool that you just carefully unpick the  threads and it loosens the button off  and we'll measure the fabric out for the  size of the patch  and then we'll fold the patch and we'll  actually mark around the area where it's  ripped we cut the patch a bit bigger  about a centimeter just in case as we're  repaying the jacket it does rip more  so we'll mark it  we'll place the jacket in the machine  and we'll edge stitch the patch  where we've done the marking  the old button has a crack in it  so we're replacing it with a brand new  button  we sew that on with a hand sewn with a  needle  there's normally four holes in the  buttons who go from each corner  crisscrossing the thread  and then we'd wind the thread around the  button to secure the button  and then slip it off of our clippers  we now check for quality and we'll hang  it on the rail ready for midline  inspection  i'm going to take it over a neil and he  will relax the jacket and bring it back  to life  firstly neil i'll pick up a scoop and  fill it with hard wax from the drum  and place it into the heated pot  the wax is melted at a temperature of no  more than 60 degrees otherwise it will  be too hot and it will go through to the  lining  we remove the garment from the rail and  position it onto the table  [Music]  we will apply some fabric into the  jacket to protect the lining  he'll now zip the jacket up  and fasten any buttons or press studs on  the front of the jacket  neil is now ready to start the rewax  process which he'll use a sponge to  apply the heated wax around the parts of  the jacket using a consistent approach  [Music]  neil uses long gliding strokes for the  application of the wax that gives it a  better finish  as knee is applying the wax you'll see  that he doesn't touch the new fabric on  the repair as that's already got a lot  of wax in it  the reeboks table is also heated at 60  degrees  so the combination of the heated wax and  the heated table helps blend the wax  into the jacket to bring it back to life  as you can see neil has now done half  the jacket and you can see the  difference of when the jacket is  reproved and when it hasn't been  reproved  neil has now finished the wax on process  and will now begin the wax off process  knee will use a dry cloth to take any  excess  wax off the jacket around the seams  studs and the buttons  finally neil will unzip the jacket and  remove any fabric he's put in to protect  the lining  check the lining is clean and is in  perfect condition  when the wax process is finished we do  allow the jacket to be hung up and dried  for  anything from 30 minutes to an hour  before we dispatch  [Music]  the jacket will now be sent out to the  customer who will be very happy with the  outcome of the jacket that's been  restored repaired and rewaxed and  they'll be able to wait for years to  come on their adventures in the outdoors  [Music]  you
 

